WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.400
We're ready for you, Your Majesty.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.860
Three, two, one.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.320
We've moved now into the '60s,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.520
away from the first two series,
which were very subdued.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:14.960
And now, we're into colour.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.040
Amy, the wardrobe designer, is brilliant.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.520
It's a huge team effort.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.800
The Queen has a different outfit
almost for every scene.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.200
And they're all handmade.
They are all incredible.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.080
By the end, we'll probably have made
about 70, 80 costumes for the Queen.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.610
And we've done over 40 for Margaret.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.960
We've done blouses, skirts,
coats, jackets,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.760
a real cross section of outfits.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.560
You can have a week or less

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.120
to turn around a costume
from start to finish.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.960
And you could fit up to ten at a time.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.240
So, it's quite concentrated.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.600
We go through every scene
to decide the feel

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.400
of what the Queen might wear
for that scene.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.025
Whether it's a state ball,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.290
it's an audience with her prime minister.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.080
That all has to be thought out.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.480
And then we decide on the design
and what we're gonna have it made in.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.960
We have a load of Queen fabrics
that we can just go to,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.800
a load of Margaret fabrics.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.360
It is that personal.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.280
The Queen's fabrics
and Margaret's fabrics,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.640
you could tell at a glance
who was who.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.960
The Queen,
I just felt she seemed more settled.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.880
So we've kept her pretty much
in a palette of sugared almond colours.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.200
Margaret is slightly more darker.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.360
Bruised, if you like, a bit like her.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.960
Enough!

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.240
There's a construct to Margaret.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.920
But it's a uniform, what she has to wear.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.280
You know, she's a married woman,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.560
but constantly looking up ways

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.640
in which she could express
her individualism.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.720
This is unlike any TV show,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.560
even any movie.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.800
It's so richly detailed.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.400
There's one Prince of Wales check suit
which I love

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.200
and is a kind of stroke of genius
from Amy, the costume designer.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.440
I don't know how many people
will pick up on the fact

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.040
that it is the Prince of Wales check.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.960
I do feel much more like Charles.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.680
Am I listened to in this family?

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.720
Am I seen for who and what I am? No.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.520
They're just so good at what they do.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.760
It's been really lovely
for something that is as big as this

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.000
to still feel like it's a family.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.240
For me to do it well, it takes
hundreds of people to do this job.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.600
So, I hope people will see a thread
of our royal family

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.360
in this huge story.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.960
We have all made sacrifices,
and suppressed who we are.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.560
It is not a choice.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.160
It is a duty.

